    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "guide": {
  "kazanim": [
   "HSAB.13 Developing simple tactics and strategies for active games (unverified): plans where to hide in hide-and-seek and how to reach home base; like Elif, suggests changing the rules of the game so that everyone can join in.",
   "HSAB.1 Applying gross motor skills effectively in different environments and physical activities — a) Performs locomotor movements in different settings and conditions: runs, hides and reaches home base at the park; notices how the ground (sand, grass, path) affects movement.",
   "MAB.1 Counting rhythmically and by sight: counts rhythmically from 1 to 7 with the seeker, matching counting with objects by touching each number card once (counting range 1–10 at 48–60 months).",
   "SDB2.2 Cooperation and D6 Honesty: renews the rules of the game by talking them over with friends; as the seeker, counts honestly without opening their eyes or peeking through their fingers."
  ],
  "once": [
   "Ask: “Have you played hide-and-seek? What does the seeker do?” Say the rhyme “Ready or not, here I come!” together.",
   "Point to Elif on the cover: “Elif is getting around the park in her wheelchair. Where do you think she could hide in hide-and-seek?”",
   "Ask: “Can the rules of a game ever change? Who changes them?” and listen to the answers."
  ],
  "sirasi": [
   "On page 1, count out loud and in rhythm together as you touch the number cards in turn: “One, two, three…”. Point out that Ali didn't peek through his fingers even though he was really curious.",
   "On page 2, look at Elif's face: “Elif felt so sad, her throat went tight. What happens in your body when you're sad?” On page 3, take a breath with Elif, in through the nose and out through the mouth.",
   "For the prediction on page 3, say that both choices are good; on page 4, have the children repeat Elif's two rules (no hiding in the sand; home base is the bench everyone can reach easily)."
  ],
  "sonra": [
   "Have the child tell the events in order: Ali was curious but counted without peeking → Elif's wheels sank into the sand → Elif took a breath and had an idea → two new rules → Elif got home free.",
   "Talk about “Ali was really curious, but why didn't he peek? What's the game like if the seeker peeks in secret?”; wait for the children to say in their own words that counting honestly makes the game good for everyone.",
   "Collect rule suggestions from the children with the question “How could we change a game in our class so that everyone can play it?”"
  ],
  "sinif": {
   "ad": "Everyone's Hide-and-Seek",
   "sure": "20 min",
   "materyal": "A garden or a big room, a chair or cushion for home base, number cards from 1 to 7",
   "adim": [
    "Choose home base together. Lay the number cards out on the floor; the seeker closes their eyes, and the children count to seven all together, pointing to the cards.",
    "Before the game, everyone suggests one rule: “No hiding in the sand”, “The seeker doesn't open their eyes”, and so on. Add the boundary rule too: “We hide where the teacher can see us, and we never get into a cupboard or a chest that closes.” Hang the rules on the board with picture symbol cards.",
    "Play hide-and-seek. A child who is found becomes the seeker's helper, so nobody is left alone.",
    "To finish, ask: “Which rule made the game more fun for everyone?” Each child answers in one sentence."
   ]
  },
  "evSoru": [
   "Ali was really curious while he counted. What did he do? Why didn't he peek at all?",
   "How did Elif calm herself down when she was sad?",
   "Which rules did Elif suggest? Which rule would you add to the games at home?"
  ],
  "evEtk": {
   "ad": "Count-to-Seven Hide-and-Seek at Home",
   "d": "Play hide-and-seek as a family at home or at the park. The seeker closes their eyes, counts out loud to seven and says, “Ready or not, here I come!” Hiding places should be within the area a grown-up can see; we never hide in places like a cupboard or a chest that closes. Before the game, add a rule together so that a little brother or sister, or the grown-ups, can join in too."
  },
  "aileNotu": "Today we read “The Hide-and-Seek Counter.” While playing hide-and-seek at the park, Elif, who uses a wheelchair, has her wheels sink into the sand and is always found first. Elif takes a deep breath and suggests two new rules for the game: no hiding in the sand, and home base is the bench everyone can reach easily. Ali, the seeker, counts to seven without peeking at all, however curious he gets. At home, while playing games, you can talk about making rules together, counting in rhythm and being honest when you're the seeker."
 },
